A. The seven-step shared plan
The panel's feedback gives us a clear next goal: make SERVE more prepared for real-world use, understand how it behaves as usage grows, and validate the idea with actual users. The panel gave us seven steps, with a clear order and ownership. The CTO can work on Steps 1–3 while the CEO starts Step 4 and both founders prepare for Step 5. Step 6 depends on what we learn in the sessions, and Step 7 checks whether either founder can present the other founder's side. The dependencies below reflect our understanding of the panel's plan, so both of us should confirm them.

Architecture diagram — CTO
Load testing — CTO
Performance optimisation — CTO
Six validation users — CEO
Six validation sessions — Both
Pitch and register — CEO
Swap demo — Both
Step 1 — Architecture diagram (lead: CTO)
Why this matters: We should both be able to explain how SERVE works, rather than relying on one founder to explain the technical side and the other to explain the business side.
What we will do: The CTO will draw one architecture diagram showing the three apps and their screens, the backend, database and external services. This includes Google sign-in, students' UPI apps and Razorpay as a possible future option. Both founders will then walk through the diagram together (see Section B).
Ownership: The CTO leads the diagram. The CEO helps by explaining what each part does for the user.
Deliverable: the diagram in cto-submission.md B.1 and the simplified drawing in ceo-submission.md D.2.
We can consider this step complete when either founder can explain every part of the diagram without help.
Evidence to keep: A dated photo of each founder's architecture drawing from memory, along with the walkthrough log in Section B.
Dependencies: None. Current status: The diagram is prepared; the joint walkthrough is still pending.

Step 2 — Load testing (lead: CTO)
Why this matters: We need to understand how much traffic SERVE can handle today and identify where performance starts to slow down.
What we will do: Run the k6 scripts in load-tests/ with 10, 50, 100, 250 and 500 virtual users. Use an isolated test backend on the target machine and run the resource monitor alongside it. Record the results using the format in cto-submission.md, Section G.5.
Ownership: The CTO runs the tests. The CEO gathers peak-hour order estimates from the canteens so that our test scenarios reflect realistic demand.
Deliverable: a baseline results table and a one-paragraph diagnosis.
We can consider this step complete when we can state the measured capacity of SERVE on a clearly identified machine.
Evidence to keep: The k6 summaries, resource-monitor CSV files, machine specifications and the exact commit that was tested.
Dependencies: Step 1, because we should understand the system before testing it. Current status: The scripts are ready and have been validated. Initial measurements were taken in a development container: the main flow handled about 163–181 API requests per second and was CPU-bound. At 50 virtual users, individual-request p95 latency was 254 ms, while the full order-flow p95 was 1.7 seconds. We still need to run the tests on the target machine.

Step 3 — Performance optimisation (lead: CTO)
Why this matters: Testing tells us where the bottleneck is. We should make a change only when the measurements give us a reason to do so.
What we will do: Choose one improvement from cto-submission.md, Section H, based on the bottleneck we measured. The current candidates relate to the CPU load of a single backend process and the database round trips involved in authentication. We will agree on the change first, implement it on a separate branch, run the test suites and repeat the load test on the same machine.
Ownership: The CTO implements and measures the change. The CEO checks that it does not make the user experience worse—for example, by delaying how quickly access is removed when a staff account is revoked.
Deliverable: a before/after table.
Completion criterion: the load test shows a measurable improvement on the main flow.
Evidence: both result sets, the commit, the decision log entry.
Dependencies: Step 2. Current status: Not started. We need both founders to approve the proposed code change first.

Step 4 — Find six validation users (lead: CEO)
Why this matters: Before we can learn from users, we need to find people who are willing to try the prototype.
What we will do: The CEO will identify six individuals from the people we have already met through our field interactions, invite them to try SERVE and schedule the sessions described in Section C.
Ownership: The CEO leads recruitment. The CTO prepares a test environment in DEMO mode and helps coordinate the sessions.
Deliverable: the recruitment tracker in ceo-submission.md E.3.
We can consider this step complete when six named individuals have agreed to take part.
Evidence to keep: A dated record of each person's agreement and whether they have given permission for us to quote their feedback. Keep these records internal.
Dependencies: None. Current status: 0 of 6 users recruited.

Step 5 — Conduct six validation sessions (both founders)
Why this matters: We want to understand what people currently do, where they face difficulties, what they need from a solution, how much time or money the problem costs them and whether they would actually consider paying.
What we will do: Run six sessions using the process in Section C. The CEO will lead the business and customer-discovery questions. The CTO will observe how people use the prototype and lead at least three of the sessions.
Responsible: both. Deliverable: six session records and the written summary (section C.6).
We can consider this step complete when all six sessions have been documented and we can identify clear patterns across the feedback.
Evidence to keep: The six session records, a bank of direct quotes and a table summarising repeated patterns.
Dependencies: Step 4. Current status: 0 of 6 sessions completed.

Step 6 — Update pitch and carry-forward register (lead: CEO)
Why this matters: Our next pitch should be based on what users actually tell us, not on assumptions. We also need to carry unresolved work into Semester 4.
What we will do: Use the quotes and numbers collected during Step 5 to update the pitch. We will also review and update the carry-forward register in Section G.
Ownership: The CEO updates the pitch and register. The CTO contributes measured technical results and any technical work that remains open.
Deliverable: updated pitch and register.
We can consider this step complete when the pitch uses genuine, traceable user evidence and the carry-forward register reflects our current priorities.
Evidence to keep: The updated pitch, with every quote linked to a session ID, and the date of the latest register review.
Dependencies: Step 5. Current status: The register has been started; the pitch update is waiting for the user sessions.

Step 7 — Swap demo (both founders)
Why this matters: We should both understand the full product and be able to handle questions outside our usual areas of responsibility.
What we will do: Rehearse both demo scenarios in Section I until each founder can present and answer questions independently.
Responsible: both. Deliverable: rehearsal log.
We can consider this step complete when both founders can complete the swapped demo without the other stepping in.
Evidence to keep: A record of two consecutive successful rehearsals for each scenario.
